WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.782
Before chunking, we perform a basic cleaning step.

00:00:03.782 --> 00:00:10.757
Our sample data is already fairly clean, so the function mostly removes repeated whitespace.

00:00:10.757 --> 00:00:13.607
Real documents can be much messier.

00:00:13.607 --> 00:00:22.607
You may see repeated page headers, navigation menus, broken line endings, OCR artifacts, duplicate text,

00:00:22.607 --> 00:00:24.452
or footer content.

00:00:24.452 --> 00:00:31.741
Cleaning matters because noisy text produces noisy chunks, and noisy chunks produce weaker embeddings.

00:00:32.786 --> 00:00:35.843
At the same time, we should not over-clean.

00:00:35.843 --> 00:00:41.293
Punctuation, headings, and sentence boundaries often carry useful structure.

00:00:41.293 --> 00:00:45.473
Removing too much can make a passage harder to understand.

00:00:45.473 --> 00:00:52.723
A good practical rule is to keep the original source text and create a cleaned version in a separate column.

00:00:52.723 --> 00:00:57.303
That gives you something to compare when retrieval quality is poor.

00:00:57.303 --> 00:01:01.448
RAG quality begins much earlier than the language model.

00:01:01.448 --> 00:01:05.007
Good source preparation is part of retrieval engineering.
